PassiveDNS on Ubuntu 26.04

These steps match Ubuntu 26.04 LTS (Resolute). libldns-dev, libjansson-dev,
and the Perl database modules are in universe. Enable that component if apt
cannot see them.

Build
-----

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install git build-essential autoconf automake \
        libldns-dev libpcap-dev

    git clone https://github.com/gamelinux/passivedns.git
    cd passivedns
    autoreconf --install

`autoreconf` comes from the autoconf package. If the shell says the
command is not found, the apt line above has not been installed yet.
There is no ./configure in a fresh git checkout until autoreconf runs.
    ./configure
    make

The binary is src/passivedns. libldns-dev on Ubuntu 26.04 is ldns 1.8.4,
which is new enough for the HTTPS and SVCB decoder (ldns 1.8.3 or newer).

    sudo install -m 0755 src/passivedns /usr/local/bin/passivedns

A source install lands in /usr/local/bin. The example init script in
etc/init.d/passivedns-debian still points DAEMON at /usr/bin/passivedns.
Change that path if you use the example.

JSON output is optional:

    sudo apt install libjansson-dev
    ./configure --enable-json
    make

Run
---

Reading a pcap does not need a capture device:

    passivedns -r capture.pcap -l /tmp/passivedns.log -L /tmp/passivedns.log

Live capture uses libpcap and starts as root. -u, -g, and -T are
fail-closed: a missing user, group, or directory makes passivedns exit.
Leave those options off until the account exists. The example in
etc/default/passivedns-debian keeps UID and GID commented for that reason.

The default BPF keeps UDP/TCP port 53, IPv4 fragments, and the IPv6
extension headers a port match would miss. It does not match 802.1Q.
For VLAN traffic pass a filter such as:

    passivedns -i eth0 -b "vlan and port 53" -l /var/log/passivedns.log

Live capture keeps 4096 bytes of each frame. -s changes it. -s 0
means 262144. A longer frame is cut, and a cut TCP DNS message is
dropped. Generic receive offload can turn a large answer into one
frame. Turn it off on the capture device:

    sudo ethtool -K eth0 gro off

passivedns warns on stderr when that offload is on. Unless -q is
set, startup prints the snapshot length and the enabled record
letters. -s does not change the snapshot length of a pcap read
with -r.

HTTPS and SVCB answers are logged by default. An empty NOERROR answer
is logged only with -X 0. -f i adds the IP TTL (IPv6 hop limit) of the
reply and is not in the default field mask. passivedns -h lists the flags.

Logs are created mode 0640. The same path can be passed to -l and -L.

Tests
-----

python3 from the base install is enough:

    python3 tests/run.py

If the binary is not src/passivedns:

    PASSIVEDNS=/usr/local/bin/passivedns python3 tests/run.py

The PHP page check runs only when php-cli is installed. Without it, that
part of the suite is skipped.

Database tools
--------------

pdns2db.pl and search-pdns.pl talk to MySQL or MariaDB. They read the
password from PDNS_DB_PASSWORD and refuse to connect without it. Optional
settings are PDNS_DB_HOST, PDNS_DB_PORT (default 3306), PDNS_DB_NAME,
PDNS_DB_USER, and PDNS_DB_TABLE (default pdns). sql/pdns.sql is that table.
A name or table that is not an identifier is refused. A port must be
1..65535. The pcre list files are operator input and are treated as trusted.

    sudo apt install libdatetime-perl libdate-simple-perl libdbd-mysql-perl

Create the database and a user with SELECT, CREATE, INSERT, and UPDATE
on it. Then:

    export PDNS_DB_PASSWORD='choose-a-password'
    perl tools/pdns2db.pl --file /var/log/passivedns.log

search-pdns.pl uses the same variables. See doc/README.

The page in www/ is a local demo. It needs php-cli and php-mysql (mysqli),
the same PDNS_DB_PASSWORD, and PDNS_WEB_TOKEN when it is reachable past
localhost.
